!!! Public APIs consumed from Matrix. The oracle checks permutations, adjacent
!!! ordering and original-index stability independently of the sorting code.
book validation.stable-index-ordering

read everything from ny.lang.core
read everything from ny.lang.collections
read everything from ny.lang.math

require receives ok as boolean, label as text returns nothing begin paragraph
    evaluate assert-true with condition being ok, message being label
end paragraph

same of type T receives actual as array of T, expected as array of T, label as text returns nothing begin paragraph
    evaluate require with ok being ((the length of actual) equals (the length of expected)), label being label
    repeat for index from 0 to (the length of actual)
        evaluate require with ok being ((actual at index) equals (expected at index)), label being label
    end repeat
end paragraph

permutation receives order as array of override integer, count as override integer returns nothing begin paragraph
    evaluate require with ok being ((the length of order) equals count), label being "permutation length"
    variable seen as array of boolean is []
    repeat for index from 0 to count
        append false to seen
    end repeat
    repeat for position from 0 to count
        value index is order at position
        evaluate require with ok being (index is at least 0 and index is less than count), label being "permutation range"
        evaluate require with ok being (not (seen at index)), label being "permutation uniqueness"
        set seen at index to true
    end repeat
end paragraph

check-i32 receives keys as array of override integer returns nothing begin paragraph
    variable original as array of override integer is []
    repeat for index from 0 to (the length of keys)
        append (keys at index) to original
    end repeat
    repeat for direction from 0 to 2
        value descending is direction equals 1
        value order is stable-sort-indices-i32 with keys being keys, descending being descending
        evaluate permutation with order being order, count being (the length of keys)
        repeat for position from 1 to (the length of order)
            value left is order at (position minus 1)
            value right is order at position
            when descending then
                evaluate require with ok being ((keys at left) is at least (keys at right)), label being "i32 descending"
            otherwise
                evaluate require with ok being ((keys at left) is at most (keys at right)), label being "i32 ascending"
            end when
            when (keys at left) equals (keys at right) then
                evaluate require with ok being (left is less than right), label being "i32 stable equal keys"
            end when
        end repeat
        evaluate same of override integer with actual being keys, expected being original, label being "i32 keys unchanged"
        append 99 to order
        when (the length of order) is greater than 1 then set order at 0 to 99 end when
        evaluate same of override integer with actual being keys, expected being original, label being "i32 result independent"
    end repeat
end paragraph

check-f64 receives keys as array of floating returns nothing begin paragraph
    variable original as array of floating is []
    repeat for index from 0 to (the length of keys)
        append (keys at index) to original
    end repeat
    repeat for direction from 0 to 2
        value descending is direction equals 1
        value order is stable-sort-indices-f64 with keys being keys, descending being descending
        evaluate permutation with order being order, count being (the length of keys)
        repeat for position from 1 to (the length of order)
            value left is order at (position minus 1)
            value right is order at position
            value a is keys at left
            value b is keys at right
            value a-nan is a does not equal a
            value b-nan is b does not equal b
            when a-nan then
                evaluate require with ok being b-nan, label being "NaNs last in both directions"
                evaluate require with ok being (left is less than right), label being "stable NaNs"
            otherwise when not b-nan then
                when descending then
                    evaluate require with ok being (a is at least b), label being "f64 descending"
                otherwise
                    evaluate require with ok being (a is at most b), label being "f64 ascending"
                end when
                when a equals b then
                    evaluate require with ok being (left is less than right), label being "f64 stable equal keys and zeros"
                end when
            end when
        end repeat
        append 99 to order
        evaluate require with ok being ((the length of keys) equals (the length of original)), label being "f64 key length unchanged"
        repeat for index from 0 to (the length of keys)
            value actual is keys at index
            value expected is original at index
            when expected does not equal expected then
                evaluate require with ok being (actual does not equal actual), label being "NaN key unchanged"
            otherwise
                evaluate require with ok being (actual equals expected), label being "f64 key unchanged"
                when expected equals 0.0 then
                    value actual-angle is atan2 with y being actual, x being minus 1.0
                    value expected-angle is atan2 with y being expected, x being minus 1.0
                    evaluate require with ok being ((actual-angle is less than 0.0) equals (expected-angle is less than 0.0)), label being "zero sign unchanged"
                end when
            end when
        end repeat
    end repeat
end paragraph

gather-ok of type T receives items as array of T, indices as array of override integer returns array of T begin paragraph
    value result is try-gather of T with items being items, indices being indices
    give result.unwrap with nothing
end paragraph

gather-missing of type T receives items as array of T, indices as array of override integer returns nothing begin paragraph
    value result is try-gather of T with items being items, indices being indices
    evaluate require with ok being (result.is-none with nothing), label being "gather rejects invalid index"
end paragraph

check-gather of type T receives first as T, second as T, third as T, label as text returns nothing begin paragraph
    value items as array of T is [first, second, third, first]
    value original as array of T is [first, second, third, first]
    value indices as array of override integer is [3, 1, 1, 0]
    value gathered is gather-ok of T with items being items, indices being indices
    evaluate same of T with actual being gathered, expected being [first, second, second, first], label being "duplicates and reordered gather"
    evaluate same of T with actual being items, expected being original, label being "gather leaves input"
    evaluate same of override integer with actual being indices, expected being [3, 1, 1, 0], label being "gather leaves indices"
    value subset is gather-ok of T with items being items, indices being [2, 0]
    evaluate same of T with actual being subset, expected being [third, first], label being "subset gather"
    value identity is gather-ok of T with items being items, indices being [0, 1, 2, 3]
    set identity at 0 to third
    evaluate same of T with actual being items, expected being original, label being "identity gather is fresh"
    set gathered at 0 to third
    evaluate same of T with actual being items, expected being original, label being "gathered writes do not reach input"
    set items at 1 to first
    evaluate require with ok being ((gathered at 1) equals second), label being "input writes do not reach result"
    set items at 1 to second
    value minimum as override integer is minus 2147483647 minus 1
    evaluate gather-missing of T with items being items, indices being [0, minus 1]
    evaluate gather-missing of T with items being items, indices being [0, 4]
    evaluate gather-missing of T with items being items, indices being [0, 2147483647]
    evaluate gather-missing of T with items being items, indices being [0, minimum]
    evaluate same of T with actual being items, expected being original, label being "rejected gather leaves input"
    value empty as array of T is []
    value empty-result is gather-ok of T with items being empty, indices being []
    evaluate require with ok being ((the length of empty-result) equals 0), label being "empty gather is Some empty"
    append first to empty-result
    evaluate require with ok being ((the length of empty) equals 0), label being "empty gather is fresh"
    value no-selection is gather-ok of T with items being items, indices being []
    evaluate require with ok being ((the length of no-selection) equals 0), label being "empty subset"
    append third to no-selection
    evaluate same of T with actual being items, expected being original, label being "empty subset is independent"
    evaluate gather-missing of T with items being empty, indices being [0]
    say label
end paragraph

main returns override integer begin paragraph
    value sizes as array of override integer is [0, 1, 2, 3, 5, 16, 17, 31, 32, 33, 257]
    repeat for size-index from 0 to (the length of sizes)
        value n is sizes at size-index
        variable integers as array of override integer is []
        variable floating-keys as array of floating is []
        repeat for index from 0 to n
            value key as override integer is (index times 37 plus 11) remainder 19 minus 9
            append key to integers
            append ((key becomes floating) over 4.0) to floating-keys
        end repeat
        evaluate check-i32 with keys being integers
        evaluate check-f64 with keys being floating-keys
    end repeat
    value minimum as override integer is minus 2147483647 minus 1
    evaluate check-i32 with keys being [2147483647, minimum, 0, minimum, 2147483647, minus 1, 0]
    evaluate check-i32 with keys being [0]
    evaluate check-i32 with keys being [minus 3, minus 2, minus 1, 0, 1, 2, 3]
    evaluate check-i32 with keys being [3, 2, 1, 0, minus 1, minus 2, minus 3]
    variable equal-keys as array of override integer is []
    repeat for index from 0 to 257
        append 7 to equal-keys
    end repeat
    evaluate check-i32 with keys being equal-keys
    value integer-ascending is stable-sort-indices-i32 with keys being [3, 1, 3, 1, 2], descending being false
    value integer-descending is stable-sort-indices-i32 with keys being [3, 1, 3, 1, 2], descending being true
    evaluate same of override integer with actual being integer-ascending, expected being [1, 3, 4, 0, 2], label being "literal ascending permutation"
    evaluate same of override integer with actual being integer-descending, expected being [0, 2, 4, 1, 3], label being "literal descending permutation"
    say "stable i32 permutations"

    value nan is sqrt with x being minus 1.0
    value infinity is exp with x being 1000.0
    evaluate check-f64 with keys being [nan]
    evaluate check-f64 with keys being [nan, nan, nan]
    value special-keys as array of floating is [nan, 2.0, minus 0.0, 0.0, minus infinity, nan, infinity, 2.0, minus 1.0]
    evaluate check-f64 with keys being special-keys
    value floating-ascending is stable-sort-indices-f64 with keys being special-keys, descending being false
    value floating-descending is stable-sort-indices-f64 with keys being special-keys, descending being true
    evaluate same of override integer with actual being floating-ascending, expected being [4, 8, 2, 3, 1, 7, 6, 0, 5], label being "literal floating ascending"
    evaluate same of override integer with actual being floating-descending, expected being [6, 1, 7, 2, 3, 8, 4, 0, 5], label being "literal floating descending"
    say "stable f64 NaN infinity zero permutations"

    evaluate check-gather of override integer with first being minus 7, second being 0, third being 2147483647, label being "gather i32"
    evaluate check-gather of override long with first being (minus 9000000000 becomes override long), second being (0 becomes override long), third being (9000000000 becomes override long), label being "gather i64"
    evaluate check-gather of override byte with first being (0 becomes override byte), second being (128 becomes override byte), third being (255 becomes override byte), label being "gather u8"
    evaluate check-gather of boolean with first being false, second being true, third being true, label being "gather bool"
    evaluate check-gather of floating with first being minus 0.5, second being 0.25, third being 1.5, label being "gather f64"
    evaluate check-gather of text with first being "", second being ("material" plus "-name"), third being "last", label being "gather text"
    value self-indices as array of override integer is [2, 0, 1]
    value self-result is gather-ok of override integer with items being self-indices, indices being self-indices
    evaluate same of override integer with actual being self-result, expected being [1, 2, 0], label being "items and indices may alias"
    set self-result at 0 to 99
    evaluate same of override integer with actual being self-indices, expected being [2, 0, 1], label being "aliased gather result is fresh"
    value special is gather-ok of floating with items being [nan, minus 0.0, infinity], indices being [1, 0, 2, 0]
    value zero-angle is atan2 with y being (special at 0), x being minus 1.0
    evaluate require with ok being (zero-angle is less than 0.0), label being "gather preserves negative zero"
    evaluate require with ok being ((special at 1) does not equal (special at 1) and (special at 3) does not equal (special at 3)), label being "gather preserves repeated NaNs"
    evaluate require with ok being ((special at 2) equals infinity), label being "gather preserves infinity"
    say "gather aliases and IEEE values"

    !!! One stable depth permutation keeps the parallel draw metadata aligned.
    value depths as array of floating is [4.5, 1.0, 4.5, nan, 2.0, 4.5]
    value draw-ids as array of override integer is [101, 102, 103, 104, 105, 106]
    value materials as array of override integer is [7, 2, 8, 9, 3, 7]
    value order is stable-sort-indices-f64 with keys being depths, descending being true
    evaluate same of override integer with actual being order, expected being [0, 2, 5, 4, 1, 3], label being "stable scene order"
    value sorted-ids is gather-ok of override integer with items being draw-ids, indices being order
    value sorted-materials is gather-ok of override integer with items being materials, indices being order
    evaluate same of override integer with actual being sorted-ids, expected being [101, 103, 106, 105, 102, 104], label being "scene IDs"
    evaluate same of override integer with actual being sorted-materials, expected being [7, 8, 7, 3, 2, 9], label being "scene materials"
    evaluate same of override integer with actual being draw-ids, expected being [101, 102, 103, 104, 105, 106], label being "scene IDs unchanged"
    evaluate same of override integer with actual being materials, expected being [7, 2, 8, 9, 3, 7], label being "scene materials unchanged"
    say "parallel scene arrays stay aligned"
    give 0
end paragraph
